    {'para': '**200.** The first persons are really subjunctive forms. The inflection of the passive imv. is precisely similar; thus, ⟨kriyai⟩, ⟨kriyasva⟩, ⟨kriyatām⟩, etc.'},
    {'para': '**201. Nouns in ⟨ṛ⟩.** These stems, like many belonging to the consonant-declension, exhibit in their inflection a difference of stem-form: strong, middle, and weak. (For the cases called strong, etc., see Introd., § 87). In the weak cases (except loc. sing.) the stem-final is ⟨ṛ⟩, which in the weakest cases is changed naturally to ⟨r⟩. But as regards the strong cases, the stems of this declension fall into two classes: in the one — which is much the larger, comprising all the *nomina agentis*, and a few others — the ⟨ṛ⟩ is vṛddhied, becoming ⟨ār⟩; while in the other class, containing'},
    {'para': 'most nouns of relationship, the ⟨ṛ⟩ is gunated, becoming ⟨ar⟩. In both classes, the loc. sing. has ⟨ar⟩ as stem-final. The abl.-gen. sing. is of peculiar formation; and the final ⟨r⟩ is dropped in the nominative singular.'},
    {'para': '**202. Nomina agentis in ⟨ṛ⟩,** like ⟨kartṛ⟩ m., ‘doer’.'},
